  "summary": "An Institute for Justice opinion piece argues that after Colorado SB 26-070 \u2014 which would have required warrants for ALPR data older than 72 hours, restricted out-of-state sharing, and capped retention at 30 days \u2014 failed in the state Senate, Colorado municipalities like Denver should pass their own ALPR safeguards. The authors cite misidentification incidents in Bow Mar, Cherry Hills Village, and Aurora, along with national reports of stalking misuse, as evidence of the need for local reform.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Colorado Senate Bill 26-070 ... would have required government agencies to obtain a warrant to access ALPR data that was more than 72 hours old, restricted ALPR data sharing outside of Colorado, and limited how long it could be retained to 30 days.",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "Following the recent controversy over Denver's ALPR contracts, calls came from members of City Council for an ordinance to create safeguards.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "In 2024, Aurora paid a $1.9 million settlement after a family with a young child was pulled over at gunpoint during a stop that reportedly involved ALPR technology when their Colorado plates were associated with a stolen motorcycle from Montana.",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    }
  ],
